WEBVTT

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:08.690
Labor: A robot specifically designed
for construction work.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.990
Labors allow dramatic advances
in construction...

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:13.700
but also in crime.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:15.950
To combat these
new Labor crimes...

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.660
the police created a special unit:
The Patrol Labor.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.540
And thus, the birth of Patlabor.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.190
yume wo sutenaide
You must not let go of your dreams

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.990
me wo sorasanaide
And never turn your gaze away

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:58.990
futari no ai mo kumoru yo
Yet even still love may cloud up and fade away

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:03.370
utsumuita kata wo
And even though you comforted your love

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.420
dakishimeta keredo
When he was feeling down

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.210
samishisa mune ni tsumotta
Sadness was still weighing down his heavy heart

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:18.430
yume wa kimi no buki no hazu da yo
Hang on tight to your dreams - they're weapons in your arsenal

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:25.020
nee da kara kanashimi mo kinukete itte
You'll find that you can get through anything at all

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:31.360
so never cry itsu mo kimi wo mitsumete 'ru yo
So never cry - you know I will always be watching over you

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:37.150
fair na ikikata no kimi ga suki
What I love about you is your strong sense of fairness

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:43.700
so never cry itsu mo kimi no soba ni iru yo
So never cry - you know I will always be right there by your side

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:49.380
da kara mata asu e kakedashite
And so now you can face your future fearlessly

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.260
New Model 97

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.740
Oh, you're still here?

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:24.910
Is that any way to say hello?

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.170
You curious?

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.080
Of course not!

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:33.380
Oh, really?

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.550
You seem happy.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:38.550
What?

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:44.260
When does the new Labor
prototype arrive?

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.720
So you are curious!

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:48.890
I'm not curious.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.190
But those kids I command are.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:54.020
Really?

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.570
Oh, are you here too, Noa?

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.910
Anything wrong with
polishing Alphonse on my day off?

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:05.870
Oh, I guess not.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.120
What are you doing here?

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.330
I thought you were
going to see a movie.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:12.290
Yeah, well...

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.460
What are you two doing here?

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:16.300
Oh, nothing.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.260
That's just pathetic!

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:23.760
We finally get a day off,
and you hang around here?

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.010
You should talk!

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.850
If I don't say it, who will?

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:30.350
The gang's all here.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:32.440
Hi.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:33.650
Hello.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.860
What's everyone doing here?

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.360
Rubbernecking.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.320
Is it that interesting,
watching me polish Alphonse?

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:44.910
Dummy!

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:50.450
Division 1's prototype Labor arrives
tonight for experimental trials.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.000
What?!

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.630
They say
it's better than the Ingram.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.420
I-I didn't know...

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.840
The specs are better,
and they have the latest technology.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:07.890
It's here! It's here!
It's here right now!

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.930
Keep going...
Keep going... Keep going...

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:19.530
It's here!

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.240
So, the grandstands are full.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.700
All right! Raise the platform
and power it up!

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.250
Begin initialization
and setup right away!

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.780
Wow. So this is the SRX-70.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.330
You can't judge it by the exterior.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.170
It comes with
a 42mm revolver cannon.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:18.830
What? That's tons
better than the Ingram!

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.290
There's more.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.760
It also has a 20mm MK 22 vulcan
revolver built into the shoulder assembly.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:28.890
It could probably
face down a military Labor.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.220
Is that good enough for you?

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:32.510
You know all about it.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.350
Why would you need so much
firepower for a police Labor?

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.150
Nothing can stand up to it!

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:50.030
He may be good, but somehow
I just don't like him.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:52.990
It's sheer power, with no soul.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:56.120
Well?

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:57.870
It's better than I expected.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:03.130
Finally, Division 1 can
show its true might!

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:06.050
I hope this is a lasting pleasure.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.510
What do you mean?
That's an odd thing to say.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.510
Are you getting carried away?

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:17.730
Captain! Please let me try out
the 42mm auto cannon!

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:19.690
You're asking the wrong person.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:26.490
Captain, you know that shooting
from a Labor can be dangerous.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:31.030
Division 1 has no experience
with heavy weapons!

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.620
I don't mean to brag...

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:38.710
but I have more experience
than anyone here!

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:40.370
That won't be necessary.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:41.920
Captain Nagumo!

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.040
Enough.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:45.090
Let me shoot the gun!
Please!

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:46.510
That's enough, I said.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.880
Please, let me shoot the gun!

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:53.680
Have you had enough?

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:55.890
No.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.650
Turn, and move to the right.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:05.650
Roger.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.880
You want the details of
how they got this new model?

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.090
Have you heard anything?

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:33.050
No. Is something bothering you?

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:35.510
Just my intuition.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:39.850
It came too quickly.
The deal sounds too good to be true.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.690
True. And oddly, Toyohata gave us
a Labor in perfect condition.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:49.320
They're also suspending payment
until the official rollout.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.610
And the manufacturer doesn't
usually handle maintenance.

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:54.660
All right. I'll check around.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.030
Thank you.

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:57.740
But, Goto, I would've thought...

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:03.000
Shinohara would be a better source
with his family connections.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:08.460
You can count on him
to do his own thing as he sees fit.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.890
I'm not the least bit jealous.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:17.850
Women's Locker Room

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:17.430
- Why not?
- I hate that Labor.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:22.730
If the police use it,
it's just going to inflame crime instead.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:27.440
And, I'm not too sure about this,
but, if it's a Toyohata product...

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.780
why is it built
by Hishii Industries?

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:31.990
That's not a big deal.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:37.910
Shinohara Industries, which built
the Ingram, used to make car parts.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:40.120
That's not what I mean.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:41.910
Shouldn't it be a company...

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:45.120
with experience
in munitions and Vulcan cannons?

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:49.170
It just seems kind of strange.
Like, can we trust them?

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.760
What if they're using Division 1
as guinea pigs?

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:55.220
Not that that's what I think.

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:58.890
Sorry to make you wait.

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:08.520
Attention pilots!

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:10.320
Stand down from
your Labors immediately!

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:14.030
Why don't you make us?

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:16.990
We're going to settle this,
once and for all!

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:18.700
Shut up, you jerk!

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.290
Settling these matters with Labors
endangers the public!

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:27.420
We don't want to get
involved in private matters.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:31.300
But settle this man-to-man,
not in Labors.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.380
Leave us alone!
We didn't ask for your opinion!

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:39.800
This thing started in a Labor,
and it's going to end in a Labor!

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:43.520
Mess with us and we'll
trash your stupid cop Labor.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:23.510
Do you still insist on
continuing this pointless fight?

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:25.520
We're sorry!

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:26.600
Please forgive us!

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:37.860
So how did your
first mission feel?

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:39.610
- Good.
- That's all?

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:42.660
- Very good.
- That's all?

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.790
How did
the auto-balance feel?

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:49.410
How was the linear actuator
and the power response?

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:52.210
Don't you have
anything else to say?

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:54.290
Is this what it's like
to pilot an Ingram?

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:56.380
Yeah, well, let's see.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:11:00.880
This may be a little
rougher than the Ingram.

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:01.640
Shige.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:08.480
This is the first time I've felt like
I hated the guys in Division 2.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:11.730
I wish I'd gotten this sooner...

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:15.860
but it's still hard
to forgive Division 2.

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:26.120
Hey! You can't
just walk in here!

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:27.410
Who are you?

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:31.620
I'm the mechanic in charge,
for whatever that's worth.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:36.290
Then you must have been informed.
You know it was mobilized today?

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:42.260
Oh, you're from the manufacturer.
We already did the maintenance.

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:43.720
That's not right.

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:47.720
The contract specifically
requires factory tolerances.

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:52.440
But you can't just
poke around in here!

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:55.900
Mr. Sakaki!
Oh, he went home.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:59.820
Captain Nagumo!
Oh, she went home.

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:02.650
Wait, you guys! Stop!

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:06.410
This looks bad, Shinobu.

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.880
Hercules 21

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:28.180
It's hard to believe,
isn't it?

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.310
It was quite a performance.

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:33.560
A thief's tactic.

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:35.060
Thief?

00:12:35.810 --> 00:12:40.440
They told Shige they were
here to adjust the equipment...

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:43.280
but their real goal
was to copy the disks.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.320
The movement pattern data.

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:58.130
Goto, are you avoiding me?

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:00.000
No.

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:03.800
You've been prying into something,
and keeping it secret from me.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:06.590
Secret? From you?

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:11.010
I have no idea what you mean.
Perhaps you're mistaken.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:13.600
Captain!

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:19.020
I looked all over for you!
Where were you hiding?

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:22.860
There's something fishy going on.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:28.910
What's wrong?
Do you have a cold?

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:31.990
Are you referring to the SRX?

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:33.620
Yes, that's right.

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:41.550
Well, I guess
the cat's out of the bag.

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:46.170
Let's move this into the office.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:57.850
I don't have all the details...

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:04.110
but Toyohata builds the SRX-70
in cooperation with Shaft Enterprise.

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:08.950
Inside the industry, it's believed
that Toyohata is a front for Shaft.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:10.910
Shaft Enterprise?

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:12.200
A conglomerate.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.120
They make everything from
toothpicks to space shuttles.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:21.130
At first I didn't connect Toyohata,
Labors, and Vulcan cannons...

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:25.090
but that Shaft surely has its
fingers in the weapons industry.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:27.300
And?

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:34.140
I'm worried that Section 2
will take the PR fallout...

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:35.560
for doing weapons development!

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:40.980
Shinohara, are you really
the heir to a Labor tycoon?

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:44.900
Captain's Office

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:47.530
I think
Nagumo finally understands.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:49.490
Huh? What exactly do you mean?

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:51.570
Captain's Office

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:55.490
Of course I'm aware
that Shaft developed the SRX.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:57.540
Why is this a problem?

00:14:57.700 --> 00:14:58.910
As you are well aware,

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:03.340
a newly assembled Labor
has very basic operating software,

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:05.000
like that of a baby.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:08.260
A Labor lives or dies
based on the patterns it learns.

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:12.260
In addition to the
constant training in Section 2...

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:16.930
our Labors acquire pattern data
in real-world combat situations...

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:19.020
including targeting
and marksmanship coordination.

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.850
And what if
Shaft steals that data?

00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:29.280
What if they were to build new products
based on the data from Division 1?

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:31.400
I thought
you would be pleased...

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:34.740
to advance the Labor industry
by making available your data.

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:39.700
Can you guarantee that our data
won't be used in waging war?

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:42.250
You're thinking
too hard about this, Nagumo.

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:43.500
Data is just data.

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:47.500
If Labors can be used for crime,
they can also prevent crime.

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:49.170
It's just that simple.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.220
Good and evil are determined
by the heart, not the tool.

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:54.640
Scientists often miss that point.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.760
It costs money
to run a Labor team.

00:15:57.220 --> 00:16:01.430
You won't get another chance
to have a state-of-the-art Labor.

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:05.110
Do you understand?
This matter has been decided.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:08.360
Will you throw away the soul
of my team for blood money?

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:17.700
Labors run on money,
not pride.

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:54.490
Captain's Office

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:31.520
There is a 402 in Tokyo Bay!
Division 1, mobilize!

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:32.230
I repeat.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:35.900
There is a 402 in Tokyo Bay!
Division 1, mobilize!

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:39.030
Don't make me say this
over and over again!

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:41.660
Hurry up,
or I'll dump you all into the sea!

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:45.080
All clear! All clear!

00:17:57.930 --> 00:17:58.680
Mr. Sakaki.

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:03.560
We're going out in the Model 97.

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:18.820
Shige, can you bring the 97s out?

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:23.280
Of course!
But what about the new model?

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:24.160
Shige.

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:28.830
I'd much rather use a model 97,
maintained by you and our crew...

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:32.960
than use a newer Labor that's
been messed around with by strangers.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:37.130
Hear that, Shige?

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:38.300
I heard!

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:42.300
Then before I dump you in the sea,
get it ready!

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:43.930
Yes, sir!

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:48.270
Call everyone together.

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:52.110
We'll be providing
backup for Division 1.

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:53.400
Yes, sir!

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:10.000
I'll fry anyone
who tries to take me!

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:20.050
A terrorist has
stolen a construction Labor.

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:22.720
He's holed up in that warehouse.

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:26.180
He's in a Hercules 21,
made by Hishii Enterprise.

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:29.230
He definitely
has a strength advantage.

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:31.900
Machines 1 and 2,
prepare to head out.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:35.320
Ready to head out!

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:42.070
Relax, Yuuki.

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:43.280
Uh, yes, sir!

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:08.810
Maybe he went underground.

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:11.980
There's a heat signature.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:13.600
I-It's behind us!

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:17.480
Yuuki!

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:22.860
Your Labor's a piece of junk!

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:34.290
Stay back!
Don't get any closer!

00:20:56.650 --> 00:20:57.610
It's over.

00:20:57.690 --> 00:20:58.860
Oh? Right.

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:40.570
I'm surprised...

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:43.360
Fukushima and the Section Chief
agreed to return the Labors.

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:48.990
He doesn't usually give in
once he's made up his mind.

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:50.950
How'd you talk them into it?

00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:52.410
You want to know?

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:54.830
Don't say
you're not going to tell me.

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:57.040
Can you keep a secret?

00:21:57.170 --> 00:21:58.290
My lips are sealed.

00:21:59.040 --> 00:21:59.830
Well...

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:02.460
I offered one condition.

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:04.550
In exchange
for accepting a new Labor?

00:22:07.550 --> 00:22:10.470
Only if we shuffle
the Divisions a bit...

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:13.510
and let Officer Ota pilot
the new Labor.

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:19.440
Let me fire it!

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:20.980
Bold.

00:22:23.020 --> 00:22:25.570
Wait! Wait! Wait for me!

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:29.530
Let me shoot the 42mm
before you go!

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:37.330
